  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "it-r3.l1",
      "title": "Ti piace la carbonara?",
      "canDo": "I can say what I like and do not like.",
      "intro": {
        "place": "cafe",
        "scene": "Friday evening in a trattoria in Trastevere. Giulia and Leo are reading the menu.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "who": "Giulia",
            "text": "Ti piace la cucina romana?",
            "meaning": "Do you like Roman food?"
          },
          {
            "who": "Leo",
            "text": "Sì, mi piace molto! Mi piacciono soprattutto i carciofi.",
            "meaning": "Yes, I like it a lot! I especially like artichokes."
          },
          {
            "who": "Giulia",
            "text": "Allora prendiamo i carciofi alla giudia. E la carbonara, ti piace?",
            "meaning": "Then let's have the fried artichokes. And carbonara, do you like it?"
          },
          {
            "who": "Leo",
            "text": "Certo! A te non piace?",
            "meaning": "Of course! Don't you?"
          },
          {
            "who": "Giulia",
            "text": "A me piace, ma non mi piacciono i piatti troppo pesanti.",
            "meaning": "I do, but I don't like dishes that are too heavy."
          },
          {
            "who": "Leo",
            "text": "Capito. Per me la carbonara, grazie!",
            "meaning": "Got it. Carbonara for me, thanks!"
          }
        ],
        "notes": [
          {
            "title": "Piacere works backwards",
            "body": "The thing you like is the subject, and you are the one it pleases. One thing: mi piace la pizza. Several: mi piacciono i carciofi. An activity: mi piace viaggiare."
          },
          {
            "title": "A me, a te",
            "body": "Mi and ti are the normal forms. \"A me\" and \"a te\" add emphasis or contrast: a me piace, a te no?"
          },
          {
            "title": "🇧🇷 PT tip: forget \"gostar de\"",
            "body": "\"Eu gosto de pizza\" tempts you into *mi piace di pizza or *io piaccio la pizza. There is no \"di\", and \"io piaccio\" means \"people like me\". Think of \"me agrada / me agradam\": the verb follows the thing."
          }
        ]
      },
