    {
      "id": "it-r3.l2",
      "title": "La prendo anch'io",
      "canDo": "I can order and refer back to food and drink with lo, la, li, le.",
      "intro": {
        "place": "cafe",
        "scene": "The waiter arrives with his notepad.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "who": "Cameriere",
            "text": "Buonasera. Volete ordinare?",
            "meaning": "Good evening. Would you like to order?"
          },
          {
            "who": "Giulia",
            "text": "Sì. Io prendo la carbonara.",
            "meaning": "Yes. I'll have the carbonara."
          },
          {
            "who": "Leo",
            "text": "Anch'io la prendo. E i carciofi, li dividiamo?",
            "meaning": "I'll have it too. And the artichokes, shall we share them?"
          },
          {
            "who": "Giulia",
            "text": "Sì, li prendiamo in due. E il vino?",
            "meaning": "Yes, we'll have them between the two of us. And the wine?"
          },
          {
            "who": "Leo",
            "text": "Lo scegli tu. Io non lo conosco bene.",
            "meaning": "You choose it. I don't know it well."
          },
          {
            "who": "Cameriere",
            "text": "Vi consiglio il rosso della casa. Lo porto subito.",
            "meaning": "I recommend the house red. I'll bring it right away."
          }
        ],
        "notes": [
          {
            "title": "Lo, la, li, le",
            "body": "They stand in for a noun you have just mentioned: lo (il vino), la (la carbonara), li (i carciofi), le (le lasagne). They go before the verb: la prendo, li dividiamo, non lo conosco."
          },
          {
            "title": "Why Italians use them all the time",
            "body": "Repeating the noun sounds heavy, and leaving the object out sounds unfinished. \"Prendo\" alone begs the question \"prendi cosa?\"."
          },
          {
            "title": "🇧🇷 PT tip: no \"ela\", no gap",
            "body": "Spoken Brazilian Portuguese says \"eu pego ela\" or just \"eu conheço\". Italian accepts neither: not *prendo lei for a dish, and not a bare \"conosco\". Use lo, la, li, le. Also remember \"subito\" is \"right away\", not PT \"súbito\"."
          }
        ]
      },
